Keep 8 bytes per argument and local in a compiled function.

A JSFunctionBytecode kept a copy of every JSVarDef of its arguments and
locals: 20 bytes each, most of them fields only the compiler reads
(scope_level, scope_next, func_pool_idx). Running code needs the name
(errors, eval), how a closure captures the variable (is_captured,
var_ref_idx) and its kind and flags. The function now keeps a
JSBytecodeVar of 8 bytes for each. A function that contains a direct
eval also keeps each one's scope (JSBytecodeVarScope, after the
records, flag has_var_scopes), which add_closure_variables reads when
the eval is compiled.

The bytecode format is unchanged apart from the new flag bit: a
function without scopes writes 0 and 0 for them. The bytecode dumps
(ENABLE_DUMPS) no longer name arguments and locals in the opcode
listing.

On Reddit's home page the function records of 32,000 functions fell
from 9.0 MB to 7.8 MB (host and PowerPC alike: the records hold no
pointers).

